From efb63a21fdca0a4819ff407c88069633ff2ad689 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 20:22:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 104/206] fix(shorebird): omit the subgraph hashes rather than
 emit wrong ones
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

subgraph_hash and op_subgraph_hash carried self_hash, which is wrong. A comment
said so; the output did not.

That is not a documented divergence, it is live bad data, and the consumer is
ours. tools/linker.py matches patch functions to base functions by
subgraph_hash and, in its own words, "unmatched functions are silently
excluded". It takes the analyzer binary as a parameter, so nothing stops it
being pointed at this one.

Measured both ways rather than argued:

  with the old fake value  build_link_table returns 1589 entries, no error
  with the keys omitted    build_link_table raises KeyError: 'subgraph_hash'

So the old behaviour would have produced a fully populated, semantically wrong
link table and no diagnostic anywhere. Omission converts that into a failure on
the first function.

⚠️ This diverges from their schema, which carries both keys, and the comment
says so. Rule 3 asks us to reproduce their interface so our output can be
checked against theirs; emitting a field whose value we know to be wrong
imitates the shape while breaking the contract, and buys a fidelity nothing can
observe at the price of a silent failure downstream. The keys come back when the
callee walk is implemented, not before.

Negative control: re-adding subgraph_hash turns class_table_dump_check red.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
index 4f9b50069ed..b257f1d2258 100644
--- a/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
+++ b/runtime/vm/analyze_snapshot_api_impl.cc
@@ -1265,11 +1265,25 @@ void Dart_DumpSnapshotInformationShorebirdAsJson(
     const std::string self_hash =
         ShorebirdSelfHash(entry.payload, entry.size);
     js.PrintProperty("self_hash", self_hash.c_str());
-    // ⚠️ NOT the real subgraph hashes. Those accumulate over the callee walk,
-    // which is unsolved -- see SUBGRAPH_HASHER_RECON.md. The keys are kept so
-    // the schema matches theirs; the values are self_hash and are wrong.
-    js.PrintProperty("subgraph_hash", self_hash.c_str());
-    js.PrintProperty("op_subgraph_hash", self_hash.c_str());
+    // subgraph_hash and op_subgraph_hash are DELIBERATELY ABSENT. They
+    // accumulate over the callee walk, which is unsolved -- see
+    // SUBGRAPH_HASHER_RECON.md -- and they used to be emitted carrying
+    // self_hash, which is wrong.
+    //
+    // Omitting beats emitting a wrong value here, and the reason is a specific
+    // consumer rather than taste. tools/linker.py matches patch functions to
+    // base functions by subgraph_hash and, in its own words, "unmatched
+    // functions are silently excluded". It takes the analyzer binary as a
+    // parameter, so pointing it at this one would have produced a quietly
+    // degraded patch with no error anywhere. A missing key raises KeyError on
+    // the first function instead.
+    //
+    // ⚠️ This is a deliberate divergence from their schema, which does carry
+    // both keys. Rule 3 asks us to reproduce their interface so our output can
+    // be checked against theirs; emitting a field whose value we know to be
+    // wrong imitates the shape while breaking the contract, and buys a
+    // fidelity nothing can observe at the price of a silent failure
+    // downstream. Restore the keys when the walk is implemented, not before.
     
     // PP slot indices
     std::vector<intptr_t> pp_slots = GetSelfPPIndices(entry.payload, entry.size);
